	<h2>Campaign Setting</h2>
	<p>Each region of Golarion is unique; culture, economy, language, and societal structure vary greatly from one part of
		the world to another. Here are some important details to know about this campaign's setting:
	</p>
	<ul style="list-style-type:circle">
		<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Click to Show Campaign and Character Details:</b></li>
		<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Click to Hide Campaign and Character Details:</b></li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Citizenship:</b> Crusaders, pilgrims, and merchants journey from across the inner sea
			to the Worldwound and its borders in answer to the call of faith and coin, but few are from here. Most adventurers
			come from the primary crusading nations or from amongst the native Kellid clansman of Sarkoris. While many
			clansman hope to reclaim the wounded lands of their ancestral home, these displaced people are without a nation,
			often claiming citizenship of Mendev as either crusading warriors or refugees. The people of Mendev make up the
			bulk of the crusaders armies, followed by the knights of Lastwall and the faithful traveling or escaping from
			Andoran, Taldor, Cheliax, and Galt. There are also a fair few people from neighboring Ustalav and Numeria, and
			nomads from Varisia, and the realms of the Horse, River, and Mammoth, Lords, though these tend to fight and travel
			in their own companies, defending their own borders and interests, rather than mingling with the crusaders.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Currency:</b> The confluence of so many nations has led to an exchange based primarily
			on weight, but nearly all coin are exchanged freely amongst the crusading towns along the Worldwound. The
			predominant currency is the Taldan standard copper, silver, gold, and platinum pennies, with nearly all standard
			coinage referred to as the same regardless of its mint origin. Alongside standard foreign currencies like
			Chelaxian silver ferries and Andoran gold sails, more exotic ones like Taldan Ha'Pennies and Crowns, Numerian
			Silverdisks, and the Dwarven trapezoidal talents can also be spotted and spent nearly as easily as anything else.
			While gold spends freely, the cursed coinage of demonkind is also used amongst them and their kin, and are not
			considered currency amongst the crusading lands; great care should be taken to not accidentally engage in the
			arbitrage of the soul trade.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Languages:</b> Because of the vast international makeup of the crusading peoples, the
			Taldan Common tongue is the most used amongst the crusaders, though the local Hallit language is spoken by most of
			the Kellids native to Mendev and Sarkoris, and is more commonly spoken in private when away from the crowds of
			city squares and markets. Abyssal is known to be the language of the enemy and is often learned by crusaders and
			cultist alike, while Celestial is the language of the church and its divine servitors, and is often heard among
			the prayers of the faithful or those trying to appear particularly devout. Draconic, Dwarven, Elven, Halfling,
			Orc, and Undercommon are also the primary language of many crusaders of diverse ethnicities. A few secret
			languages (such as druidic), ancient languages, and magical communication - particularly <i>telepathy</i> and
			<i>speak with animals</i> - will both feature prominently, but cannot be achieved by all characters. Many powerful
			beings expect to be able to communicate through their favorite mediums, including magical means, and may feign
			incomprehension of languages they do not wish to converse in, while some may understand a language but not be able
			to speak it at all. Other useful languages for this campaign include: Aklo, Giant, Ignan, Infernal, Necril,
			Osiriani, Shadowtongue, Sylvan, and Terran, but these will not be as prominent. While there remain more languages
			that may feature incidentally, they are unlikely to be the ideal choice when choosing a new one to learn.
		</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Prejudice:</b> While all are welcome to take up arms in the crusade against the Abyss,
			the tragedies of the Red Morning Massacre and the inquisitions of the Third Crusade cast a long shadow in peoples
			memory. Tieflings, witches, the insane, and the deformed bear the brunt of this discrimination, out of fear that
			their heritage, pursuits, or poor health make them naturally more susceptible to the influence of the enemy. Other
			outsider-blooded adventurers, occult practitioners, summoners (or god-callers as the Sarkorians call them), and
			followers of deities perceived to be less-righteous or less-zealous than the crusading faiths also bear a great
			deal more scrutiny, and these may all need to put up with enhanced security checks to prove they are not possessed
			or directed by demonic intent. Even the penitent few who have once looked upon the heart of the Abyss and turned
			away are often scorned, as the suspicion of heresy never truly fades. While this prejudice is rooted in fear, it
			is often justified as necessary vigilance for ensuring the safety and security of the crusaders, civilians, and
			refuges living along the Worldwound.</li>
	</ul>
</section>